Z||000000||fierolarry||03-14-2001||10:26 AM||||Hi everybody. Ever since I bought my 87 GT a year ago i've had problems with my radio. When the car has been sitting for awhile thyheradio & tape player would work fine but after awhile the sound would stop coming on the radio was on but no sound.After I had the alternator replaced I had no problems for more than a month. Then it started doing it again Now i hardly ever get sound and only for a short period of time. any ideas?The radio has the equalizer and sub-woofer<BR>It is the stock radio w/ no alterations that i know of.-it has the stock radio with no alterations that I know of----------------<P><BR>[This message has been edited by fierolarry (edited 03-14-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by fierolarry (edited 03-15-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by fierolarry (edited 03-15-2001).]<P>[This message has been edited by fierolarry (edited 03-15-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by fierolarry (edited 03-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||KRMFiero||03-14-2001||10:48 AM||||loose connections maybe? i'm not sure...   try borrowing a radio from someone and put it in see if there is a difference<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||tgowens||03-14-2001||03:44 PM||||intermittent problems like that are indicative of a faulty connection or short in the wiring -- you didn't say if it is the stock radio or an aftermarket one.......||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-14-2001||08:14 PM||||If you are reasonably sure the speakers are good and the connectors are in all the way on the back of the radio.....<P>Delco had minor production problems with the radios. As a result the main (large) connector in the radio has a habit of cracking loose from the circuit board. It can be fixed but you have to take the radio pretty far apart to do it. (This affected allot of Delco radios in the 80's.)<P>This shows up in a few diferant ways. one or more speakers cutting out at the same time. display going out when headlights are on. whole radio shuts off.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Beaverman||03-14-2001||09:55 PM||||Perhaps if your running an amp on your speakers...including the subwoofer...the 40 bus fuse is fried on your amp.  If not maybe the outs on your radio went bad...||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierolarry||03-15-2001||11:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by tgowens:<BR><B>intermittent problems like that are indicative of a faulty connection or short in the wiring -- you didn't say if it is the stock radio or an aftermarket one.......</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||03-15-2001||07:30 PM||||I'd still say the main connector on the radio is flaky. If the OE sub amp itself had a problem you would only loose the sub. not all the other speakers. I'd also check the harness going to the sub amp.<P>with the car parked and the key on.... A quick test is to reach under the dash and wiggle the main connector on the back of the radio. you should be able to reach it. slide straight back along the left side of the radio shroud. as you reach behind the shoud the connector should be right there easy to get to. If the speakers go in and out as you wiggle the connector then you've got very likely to tear the radio apart and fix the connector or replace the radio.<P>Alternatly you can unscrew the radio from the dash and pull it out where you can see. Note that the harness will move as you pull out the radio so the thing may work just by being moved.||||reg||1
